 These are the points to agree on with the other groups before testing together.
+
+## 10. UDP discovery (new in M5)
+
+Lets a client find chat servers on the local network without typing an address.
+
+**Transport.** UDP, server port **12346** by default (`--udp-port`). UDP and TCP
+ports are separate spaces; we use a different number anyway to avoid confusion.
+UDP keeps message boundaries, so each datagram is one complete message: no `\n`
+framing is needed (a trailing `\n` or spaces are allowed and ignored).
+
+**Request** (client → broadcast or a specific host, UDP port 12346). Any of:
+
+```
+{"type":"DISCOVER"}
+DISCOVER
+```
+
+The server MUST accept both (case-insensitive), because the assignment does not
+fix the format. Our client sends both, to every destination.
+
+**Reply** (server → the request's source address and port, unicast):
+
+```
+{"type":"SERVER","port":12345,"name":"java-chat-ana"}
+```
+
+- `port` (integer): the server's TCP chat port. `name` (string, optional): a label
+  to show in a list.
+- The reply contains **no IP address**: the client uses the *source address of the
+  reply datagram*. That is always an address that reached the client, even when the
+  server has several interfaces (Wi-Fi, Ethernet, VPN) or does not know its own IP.
+- A server MUST NOT answer anything that is not a DISCOVER (in particular not other
+  servers' replies), so two servers can never start a reply loop.
+
+**Client behaviour.** Send the request to `255.255.255.255`, to every interface's
+directed broadcast address (e.g. `192.168.1.255`; macOS sends `255.255.255.255`
+out of one interface only) and to `127.0.0.1`; collect replies for 1.5 s; drop
+duplicates. Our client also understands replies from other groups in other
+shapes: JSON with `port` as number or string, or plain text containing a port
+number (`PORT 12345`, `12345`).
+
+**When it does not work.** Broadcast never crosses routers, so all machines must be
+on the same subnet. University and hotel Wi-Fi often block broadcast or isolate
+clients from each other; a phone hotspot or a lab switch works. The macOS firewall
+must allow incoming connections for `java`. TCP still works with `--host <ip>`.
